Subject: RegExp: a greedy loop over one character runs as a single backtrack frame; ASCII case folding inline

What this does
  - libregexp-backtrack.c.inc: a greedy `X*` or `X+` whose X is one character matcher (dot, any, \s, \S, a literal, a class, and their case-insensitive forms) no longer saves a state per iteration. The matcher scans the whole run in a tight loop (a 256-bit bitmap for a class over an 8-bit subject of 16 or more characters) and saves one four-slot frame holding the run start, the exit pc and the current end. Backtracking into it gives back one character (a surrogate pair in Unicode mode) per failure and drops the frame at the run start. The lookahead state copy and the negative-lookahead pop know the four-slot frame.
  - libregexp.c: lre_canon_fast does the ASCII case of lre_canonicalize inline; the char_i, range_i and run loops use it. REOP_range shares lre_range_has with the run loop.
  - The run is chosen at run time from the bytecode shape (the atom is followed by the goto to its own split, or precedes the split of an X+ tail); the bytecode itself is unchanged.

Why we need it
  The matcher saved and popped one state, tested the stack space and polled the interrupt counter for every character of `[^;]*`, `\s*`, `\w+` and `.*`. The URL regexp, the trim regexps and the cookie scan of the Octane regexp row are loops of this shape.

Failure modes
  No struct shared with quickjs.c changes, but libregexp.o must be rebuilt along with quickjs.o: a stale object silently lacks the change.
  Captures inside the loop body, counted loops, lazy loops and loops with a zero-advance check keep the generic path.

